  "texts": [
    "Why does a spoon in tea burn your fingers?",
    "Fresh tea is around eighty degrees Celsius.",
    "Heat is really just atoms jiggling around.",
    "Hot tea makes the atoms at the spoon's tip shake.",
    "They nudge their neighbors, passing the motion along.",
    "Metal also has free electrons that roam around.",
    "They carry heat along the spoon really fast.",
    "Soon, the handle gets hot too.",
    "Steel conducts heat about a hundred times better than wood.",
    "Wood has no free electrons, so heat just crawls.",
    "Metal also dumps heat into your fingers very quickly.",
    "Above forty-five degrees, your nerves start signaling pain.",
    "Same reason metal feels so cold in winter.",
    "That's why pots have handles made of plastic or wood.",
    "Follow for more, and stay burn-free next time!"
  ]
